    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/volubilis"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/volubilis.jpg" alt="Volubilis" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/volubilis">Volubilis</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Volubilis_Longshot_II.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Subhros, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>By the second half of the 8th century the far <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maghreb">Maghreb</a> had been effectively independent of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphs</a> since the Kharijite-led Berber revolts of 739–40, and neither the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyads</a> nor the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasids</a> after 750 regained control of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a>. The land was held by local <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> tribes and principalities such as the Barghwata Confederacy on the Atlantic coast and the Midrarid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">Emirate</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sijilmasa">Sijilmasa</a>. Into this country came <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/idris-i-of-morocco">Idris ibn Abdallah</a>, a great-grandson of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hasan-ibn-ali">Hasan ibn Ali</a> and so a descendant of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ali-ibn-abi-talib">Ali ibn Abi Talib</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatima">Fatima</a>, the daughter of the prophet Muhammad. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-fakhkh">Battle of Fakhkh</a>, near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a>, between the Abbasids and the supporters of Muhammad's descendants, he fled west, reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tangier">Tangier</a> and by 788 had settled at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volubilis">Volubilis</a>, Walili in Arabic.</p><p>The powerful <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/awraba">Awraba</a> Berbers of Volubilis, Muslims in a land where most tribes were Christian, Jewish, Kharijite or pagan, took Idris in and made him their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imam">imam</a>, seeing in a Sharifi leader a way to strengthen their own position. Idris I asserted his authority, worked to subdue the Christian and Jewish tribes, and in 789 founded Medinat Fas south-east of Volubilis. In 791 he was poisoned by an Abbasid agent. He left no male heir, but his wife Lalla Kanza bint Uqba al-Awrabi bore his only son shortly after his death; his Arab freedman Rashid raised the boy and held the regency for the Awraba until the Abbasids killed him in 801. The next year <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/idris-ii">Idris II</a>, aged eleven, was proclaimed imam.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/qarawiyyin-mosque"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/qarawiyyin-mosque.jpg" alt="Qarawiyyin Mosque" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/qarawiyyin-mosque">Qarawiyyin Mosque</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:University_of_Al_Qaraouiyine.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Abdel Hassouni, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Idrisids were principally rulers of towns and had little power over most of the country's people, who went on speaking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berber-languages">Berber languages</a> and often following heterodox Islamic doctrines. Their achievement was urban. Idris I had founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fez">Fez</a> as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> market town; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/idris-ii">Idris II</a> developed it, moved his government there in 809 and founded the settlement of al-'Aliya, and welcomed two waves of Arab immigration, one from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a> in 818 and another from Aghlabid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunisia">Tunisia</a> in 824, which gave Fez a more Arab character than other <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maghreb">Maghrebi</a> cities. Under his successors Islamic and Arabic culture took hold in the towns, more Arab immigrants arrived in the reign of Yahya I (849–863), and the famous mosques of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qarawiyyin-mosque">al-Qarawiyyin</a> and al-Andalusiyyin were founded. Fez flourished and became an important religious centre.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a> profited in these years from the trans-Saharan trade, which came to be dominated by Muslim, mostly Berber, traders. The Idrisid towns formed part of the wider network that linked the Maghreb with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahara">Sahara</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudan">Sudan</a> to the south, and the Muslim merchants who controlled it in the 9th century carried <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> with their goods. Beyond the walls, the countryside remained the Berbers' own: the vast majority of the population lived outside the Arabized cities, and the Idrisid state never brought the principalities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sijilmasa">Sijilmasa</a>, Barghawata and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nekor">Nekor</a> under its control.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/idris-ii"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/idris-ii.jpg" alt="Idris II" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/idris-ii">Idris II</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dirham_of_Idris_II,_828_A.D.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, CC BY-SA 2.5</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Idris I spread his authority across much of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a>, as far as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tlemcen">Tlemcen</a>, but he was wholly dependent on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/awraba">Awraba</a> leadership. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/idris-ii">Idris II</a> broke that dependence by welcoming Arab settlers at Walili and appointing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a> as his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">vizier</a> and qadi; when the Awraba leader Ishak plotted against his life with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aghlabid-dynasty">Aghlabids</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunisia">Tunisia</a>, Idris had his former protector killed and in 809 left Walili for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fez">Fez</a>. The wars that followed his death in 828 were mostly fought within the family. Isa rose against his brother Muhammad and was driven into refuge at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chellah">Chellah</a> by Umar, who then turned on al-Qasim for refusing to join them and was rewarded with the governorship of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tangier">Tangier</a>.</p><p>The dynasty's enemies multiplied in the late 9th century. In 868 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> Khariji Sufri tribes of Madyuna, Ghayata and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/miknasa">Miknasa</a> of the Fez region, led by Abd al-Razzaq, formed a common front, defeated Ali ibn Umar from their base at Sefrou and occupied Fez, though the city refused to submit and Yahya III retook it and went on attacking the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufriyya">Sufris</a>. Yahya III himself died in battle in 905 against his kinsman Yahya ibn Idris ibn Umar. In the 10th century the Idrisids became the quarry of Zenata armies fighting for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatimid-caliphate">Fatimids</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyads</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a>: the Miknasa under Masala ibn Habus took Fez in 917, Musa ibn Abul 'Afiya besieged the family at the fortress of Hajar an-Nasr, and after breaking the siege the Idrisids defeated the Miknasa troops and later pursued and killed Musa himself.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/chellah"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/chellah.jpg" alt="Chellah" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/chellah">Chellah</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Roman_Ruins_Zawiya_Chellah_Rabat_Nov25_A7CR_08989.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">This Photo was taken by Timothy A. Gonsalves. Feel free to use my photos, but please mention me as the author. …, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Idrisid ruler bore the title of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imam">imam</a>, conferred on Idris I by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/awraba">Awraba</a> and on his son by the same tribe in 802. Whether the dynasty was Shi'i is disputed. Idris I had Shi'ite sympathies and, as Encyclopædia Britannica puts it, the state founded by his son was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni</a> in matters of religious doctrine; primary sources and contemporary scholars describe the Idrisids as Sunni, while others have called them Shi'a or Zaydi because of their political opposition to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasids</a>. The Awraba who welcomed Idris were Mu'tazila, and he relied widely on Mu'tazila <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> tribes, though how far his community kept that character is unclear.</p><p>Succession was the dynasty's weakness. On <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/idris-ii">Idris II</a>'s death in 828 his son Muhammad divided the kingdom among seven of his brothers, forming eight Idrisid statelets in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a> and western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a>: Muhammad ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fez">Fez</a> with only nominal power over the rest, al-Qasim <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tangier">Tangier</a>, 'Umar the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanhaja">Sanhaja</a> and Ghumara of the Rif, Isa the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tamesna">Tamesna</a> coast around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chellah">Chellah</a>, Yahya Hisn Dai, Hamza Walili and 'Ubayd <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allah">Allah</a> the south with the Lamta tribes and Tamdult, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agadir">Agadir</a>, later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tlemcen">Tlemcen</a>, remained with the Sulaymanid cousins. Ali ibn Muhammad (836–849) and Yahya I (849–863) ruled competently and the state was stable, but Yahya II divided the realm again and died in uncertain circumstances in 866 after fleeing his palace. The ruling line passed from the sons of Muhammad to Umar's son and then to al-Qasim's.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Idrisid period played an important part in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islamization">Islamization</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a>, carrying <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> from the northern coast, where the first Muslim presence had been concentrated, to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> tribes of the interior. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fez">Fez</a> became a centre of Arabization, and through the trans-Saharan trade that the Idrisid towns joined, and the Muslim merchants who came to dominate it, Islam spread onward to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudan">Sudan</a>. The tombs of Idris I at Moulay Idriss, near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volubilis">Volubilis</a>, and of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/idris-ii">Idris II</a> in Fez grew into important religious complexes and pilgrimage sites, such as the Zawiya of Moulay Idris II.</p><p>The dynasty's deepest legacy was the precedent of Sharifian rule, government by descendants of the Prophet, which inspired the rise of Sharifism in Morocco in the 15th century and is maintained by the country's present ruling dynasty, the 'Alawis. The Idrisids spawned many sharifian families; in the 11th century one branch descended from Umar, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hammudid-dynasty">Hammudids</a>, won power in several cities of northern Morocco and southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>. From the 14th century local writers portrayed the Idrisids as the starting point of an Islamic Maghrib al-Aqsa, the Furthest West; Ibn Abi Zar's Rawd al-Qirtas promoted continuity between Idrisid and Marinid rule, and both the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marinid-dynasty">Marinids</a> and the Wattasids sought at times to associate themselves with the dynasty. In modern Morocco's national narratives the Idrisids are often the first Moroccan dynasty and the beginning of an unbroken tradition of monarchy.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The dynasty's power declined slowly from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/idris-ii">Idris II</a>'s death in 828 and the division of his realm. Ali ibn Umar, who took power after Yahya II's death in 866, was beaten in 868 by the Sufri Kharijite front, which held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fez">Fez</a> until Yahya III retook it. Yahya III fell in battle in 905 against Yahya IV, a grandson of Umar. In 917 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/miknasa">Miknasa</a> under Masala ibn Habus, acting for their Fatimid allies, attacked Fez and forced Yahya IV to recognize Fatimid suzerainty, then deposed him in 919 or 921. Hassan I al-Hajam, a grandson of al-Qasim, held Fez from 925, but in 927 Musa ibn Abul 'Afiya returned, captured and killed him; the Idrisids never held Fez again.</p><p>The family took refuge at Hajar an-Nasr in the north. When Musa turned to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyads</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a> in 931 and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatimid-caliphate">Fatimids</a> sent Humayd ibn Yasal against him, the Idrisids broke the siege and later killed Musa. Settled among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jbala">Jbala</a> tribes of the Rif, they partly rebuilt their power, acknowledging now Córdoba, now the Fatimids: al-Qasim al-Gannun ruled for the Fatimids from 938 to 948; his son Abul-'Aysh turned to the Umayyads and left for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-andalus">al-Andalus</a> in 954; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jawhar-al-siqilli">Jawhar</a>'s invasion of 958 restored Fatimid overlordship. In 973 the Umayyad general Ghalib invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a>, expelled the Idrisids and in 974 took al-Hasan ibn al-Qasim al-Gannun and many of his kin as hostages to Córdoba. Al-Hasan's return with Fatimid support in 985 ended in defeat and his assassination on the road to Córdoba.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Idrisid dynasty, founded when the Alid refugee Idris I was made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imam">imam</a> by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/awraba">Awraba</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berbers</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volubilis">Volubilis</a> in 788, built <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fez">Fez</a> into the Arab and Islamic centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a> and ruled most of the country until division among <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/idris-ii">Idris II</a>'s heirs, Kharijite revolt and the proxy wars of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatimid-caliphate">Fatimids</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyads</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a> ended its power in 974.</em></p>

